1, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*"
.\_scratchpad\panel_sgirekills_pass2_20260908\codex.err:3205:  "state_note": "OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED\nHERE.  ALL THREE WERE. ***\n\n**1. THE CLASS COUNT IS WRONG IN THE HEADLINE, FOR THE FOURTH TIME ON THIS ROW.**  `grep -cE\n'^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` gives 12 STATEMENTS, but `:1537` **cannot run**: it is the trailing `else`\nof `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal; exit(1); }` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)` --\nan exhaustive pair with a third arm.  Already filed as [[sgimtewidth]].\n\n> **ELEVEN guest-reachable kills, in two classes: 6 DIRECTION + 5 MTE-feature.**  This row has now\n> carried 21 -> 19 -> 12 -> 11, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*"
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4668:as ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`&#x27;s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4795:*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4819:&gt; `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4821:**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4823:could look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:5435:**CORRECT, AND NOW EVIDENCED.**  memory_rw.c calls `mem-&gt;devices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, writeflag, extra)&#x27; ONCE, with `len&#x27; passed through.  There is no loop around it.  `len&#x27; is written in exactly two places: a PAGE-STRADDLING split path, and a clamp to the device&#x27;s own end which prints a diagnostic.  An aligned halfword at 0x0002 neither straddles a page nor reaches the device end, so it arrives as ONE call with len == 2.  The leg holds -- but glm was right that I had asserted it, and this is the second time today a seat caught me presenting an untraced leg as settled. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260901_183841_pcc2p1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-01 — MANUAL — not ratifiable as written; convert the #119 sites; names the escaping mutant.  Q1 decline + latched announce with a PER-INSTANCE bit.  Q2 addition licence NOT satisfied without one further fact.  Q3 **CONVERT** the two `#119&#x27; sites rather than leaving the file with two latch idioms.  Q4 the smallest escaping mutant is `|=&#x27; -&gt; `=&#x27; in a per-arm bitmask -- which is the same family as the flood mutant a sibling round measured today (`|=&#x27; dropped), and confirms that the latch&#x27;s ASSIGNMENT operator is the fragile part, not its test. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260901_183841_pcc2p1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-01 — MANUAL — same leg, independently; and the licence warrant is insufficient.  Reached the device-dispatch gap independently of glm.  Also: the Q2 warrant is insufficient under the addition licence **because dev_pcc2 is SINGLE-INSTANCE** -- so the per-instance-latch argument (one instance silencing another) has no instance to bite on here, and the transferred warrant does not transfer cleanly.  That is the sharpest form of the objection and it is not answered by pointing at the house idiom. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260901_183841_pcc2p1</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — Self-declared claude-fable-5-1 (a claim). *** THE TREE ALREADY KNEW WIDE ACCESS IS LEGAL: mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10 says PCC2 registers can be accessed as 8/16/32 bits -- neither the brief nor the 2026-09-02 adjudication cited it. Refutes FAULT; reframes DECLINE as refusing a hardware-legal access. *** DELETE is measurably WRONG for writes: memory_readmax64 composes big-endian so (uint8_t)idata is the HIGHEST-address lane -- st.h 0xAABB to GENCTL would store VECBASE&#x27;s byte. Mandatory mutant + readback row. *** THE SEVEN ODD ARMS ARE OPERATOR-REACHABLE: debugger_cmds.c warns on a misaligned put and then STORES, through memory_rw to the handler. Fix all identically; contract states 5 guest-reachable / 7 operator-reachable. *** IPL is at 0x3e (EVEN) -- its #119 arm is guest-reachable and prints ignored about a read the memcpy already served; fold IPL/MASK in (12 bits). Corrected its OWN 2026-09-02 ruling in three places. Unnamed option member: COMPOSE (per-lane dispatch), with the hazard that it must live INSIDE the arms. C040 kill (legal byte write 0x04 to GENCTL -&gt; exit) has no ledger row. Ten arms / twelve labels. ALL SIX CHECKABLE CLAIMS VERIFIED BY THE MAIN LOOP. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — UNKNOWN — *** SEAT DOWN: API 529 Overloaded on claude-opus-5, twice (first firing + the one permitted re-fire). Design pass -- a READING lost, not a measurement; the other seats carry it. NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — Ten blocks covering twelve labels. Precedent argument AGAINST fixing the odd arms: OB sgirekills says an unreachable terminating site must not be fixed without an executable row -- BUT that case was a dead else-arm no input can reach, a different species from arms the debugger reaches; the operator-reach measurement decides it. Do NOT copy #497&#x27;s 1&lt;&lt;N enum mechanically (the fork records it as collision-prone); use auto-increment + NBITS. Full decline-side row set: pre/post byte snapshots, one message per fresh instance, same-site-twice, five sites once each, TWO INSTANCES -&gt; two messages to distinguish per-instance from static. Two separate findings: the counter arm accepts 8-byte writes that raw-copy through PSCALE and both timer-control bytes; valid aligned wide starts at 0x14/0x18/0x2c hit the default kill (-&gt; pcc2defaultkill). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — UNKNOWN — [NOT-FIRED] *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling; spent on the concurrent pass-14 panel as its builder instead. NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — UNKNOWN — *** SEAT DOWN, TOOL FAILURE, TWICE: first firing died after 165 bytes with the CLI&#x27;s own &#x27;Internal error:&#x27;; the one permitted re-fire ran 3 turns / 590 s and exited rc=1 with 478 bytes of narration and no verdict. It confirmed &#x27;ten exit(1) width-kill blocks&#x27; in passing. No quota text, so not a wall; a grok.exe process was alive throughout, so not a stall -- a CLI failure class. NOT agreement. The pcc2 pass-1 roster is therefore 6 of 9 answering (grok x2 tool failure, opus x2 API 529, agy reserved). *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — Fix all arms: uniformity makes a STATIC gate row expressible (grep -c exit(1) -&gt; only default remains), and the reachability proof is an inference future CPU work could silently invalidate. Value rows for reads (composed value, right lane); decline-no-store + latch-once + neighbour-preservation for writes. Found: the C040 byte-write kill survives this fix and must be cited or ten-removed reads as file-clean; pcctwo_interrupt_common&#x27;s default exit is not guest-reachable today; three latch idioms will coexist post-fix and the contract must say why. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — MANUAL — Packet-fed. *** P5 WRONG IN BOTH CLAUSES, VERIFIED BY THE MAIN LOOP: the byte arms are NOT reg = idata -- VECBASE &amp; 0xf0, TxICR |= (idata &amp; 0x17) + pending clears, SCCERR = 0, SCC* &amp; 0x1f/0xdf -- and reassert_interrupts is in SIX arms, not two. *** And it derived the cleanest justification of the split: the read path uses raw data in address order (width-correct by construction); every write arm uses idata, which means the addressed byte only at len==1. Reads: delete the discard. Writes: cannot be fixed without per-arm semantics. Decline follows. Two more unnamed members: first-byte-only accept; FAULT&#x27;s feasibility not in the packet (what does the caller do with return 0?). The witness cannot protect the fix: no byte-write row, no value rows. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_093835</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — MANUAL — Packet-fed. Ten blocks / twelve registers conflated; premise 4&#x27;s already-correct unproven (lane order,