.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:6143:is how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-06 — GPT-6 Astra, max effort, on a purpose-written assess brief (answer preserved at _scratchpad/codex_lcabadval/ANSWER_astra_20260906.txt; refused the first packet on its content filter and answered the neutralised one).  **IT CORRECTED THE ROW&#x27;S CENSUS AND MY BRIEF&#x27;S, AND I VERIFIED THE SHARPEST CORRECTION BY OPENING THE FILE.**  Comment-aware counting gives **12 textual exits, 11 LIVE**: six value guards, two interrupt, one nonzero-bus, one ISA-range, one IOC-default.  **The `dev &gt;= 21&#x27; guard my brief listed as a live range kill is COMMENTED OUT** -- dev_lca.c has `/*  fatal(...); exit(1);  */` inside it and assigns `dev = 0;` instead.  Verified.  So the brief asked about a dead guard, and the premise-audit opener is what surfaced it.  Also: exactly THREE guards accept a single value (not four), and FOUR diagnostics carry the phrase `from the only implemented&#x27;.  **IT ALSO REFUTED THE CONFOUND AS I STATED IT**, which changes the witness design rather than the defect: `Any interrupt overstates the confound&#x27; -- bus_isa.c (anchor `old_isa_assert == new_isa_assert&#x27;) filters unchanged masked state, so only callbacks that actually PROPAGATE terminate.  The oracle still cannot rest on exit status, but the interference is narrower and testable.  Its witness specification: drive the named IOC cases through the real memory decode with native quadword accesses, and require same-decode liveness, a device-specific signature beyond an ordinary write/read echo, independent value and width recording, state preservation, and latch repetition plus instance controls -- with the oracle validated against separate interrupt-diagnostic fixtures.  It found NO Alpha or LCA regression probe anywhere in the tree, which is why this file has no coverage at all.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row&#x27;s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed decode or does the emulator not model them?  Both were asserted. ***  The source settles it against me: base/mask/tlb_enable are written and read back and nothing else; only `ioc_conf` is consumed (`bus = d-&gt;ioc_conf &amp; 1`).  **That splits the fix design** -- three arms are pure storage where a decline may keep the store, and the fourth is not.  Also the sharpest design point of the panel: for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the MAXIMALLY ORDINARY write, so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one.  Named `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` as the single fact that would close the census, which is exactly the right move for a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would settle whether these are hardware-legal values or merely the ones this emulator was developed against.  There is no such document in `_scratchpad/`. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row&#x27;s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm&#x27;s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth&#x27;s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger&#x27;s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.  QUESTION (added 2026-09-08, after codex settled the disposition and left the hardware claim open): The DISPOSITION is now settled by codex against #447&#x27;s actual implementation: decline the out-of-set write and PRESERVE the previous value (the queue&#x27;s option B), with the store moved BELOW the guard -- because today the store runs FIRST (dev_lca.c:233 vs the guard at :236), so merely deleting the exit(1) leaves the bad value STORED and guest-readable.  What is NOT settled, and is this row&#x27;s research question: **what does the real 21066 LCA do with an out-of-set value in these six registers?**  We have NO LCA datasheet on this machine -- `_scratchpad/*.pdf` holds none -- and the only primary source is the OpenBSD 2.2 driver (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c`), which shows what a GUEST writes and not what the CHIP accepts.  So: (a) is decline-and-preserve defensible as MODELLING, or is it merely the safest thing to do in ignorance -- and should the round SAY the latter rather than imply the former?  (b) The guard&#x27;s accepted sets are themselves unverified: W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 REJECT their own reset value of 0, and the header names 0 as IOC_W_MASK_1M, which suggests 0 is a legal encoding the whitelist omits.  **Is the correct round actually to widen or delete the whitelists rather than to change what happens when they fail?** — evidence: [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none&#x27; would have meant `did not think to ask&#x27;. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null&#x27;; machine_alpha.c&#x27;s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,&quot;lca&quot;).  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279&#x27;s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh&#x27;s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)&#x27; = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -&gt; memory_rw.
.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:6145:*** THE CONFOUND, and it is why an exit-status oracle here is worthless -- VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c: `lca_interrupt_assert&#x27; and `lca_interrupt_deassert&#x27; are BARE fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert OR deassert ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test. ***  Every row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM.  The two tick candidates are quiescent at a cold prompt (8253 constructed with in_use=0; ns16550 asserts only with com_ier bits set), so it is controllable -- do not touch the ISA window. — evidence: agent seat, read-only; claims re-checked against dev_lca.c by the main loop</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-09-07 — CONFIRMED — CODEX FIX-CHECK on the six-arm proposal, 2026-09-07.  VERDICT: **Qualified yes** -- &quot;the six stores and your consumer analysis check out ... the sibling precedent does not establish hardware correctness or close the remaining termination paths.&quot;  Artifact: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_fixcheck_20260907.txt.  Brief: _scratchpad/brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md -- a CHECK-MY-CONSTRUCTION brief, with the seat explicitly told not to design a probe or choose register values (the C5 scoping rule).
