13593: ### `lcabadval` (HIGH) -- four registers accept exactly one value each and kill on the rest
13594: 
13595: ```c
13596: if (idata != 0x700000ULL) {
13597:         fatal("... (0x%" PRIx64") from the only implemented value
13598: ", idata);
13599:         exit(1);
13600: }
13601: ```
13602: 
13603: Four of these �?" `idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`, `!= 0x700000ULL`,
13604: `!= 0x240000000ULL`, `!= 0x3ff00000ULL` �?" plus two single-bit masks (`idata & ~1`,
13605: `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`). Eleven live terminating calls in the file.
13606: 
13607: **The #443 BADVAL class**, the same shape as `gdromcondlies` (rated HIGH, since fixed) and
13608: `sgideprimop` (filed HIGH tonight). The author documented the decision rather than hiding it �?"
13609: each diagnostic says *"from the only implemented value"*. An honest TODO expressed as a host kill.
13610: 
13611: > **A guest here is FIRMWARE** �?" SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset �?" so the reaching value is
13612: > whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice. A model implementing one value and
13613: > dying on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.
13614: 
13615: **Scope, stated so it is not over-claimed:** the two *range* guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`)
13616: are a different question and are **not** analysed here. A bounds guard that kills may be
13617: protecting a real array, and deciding that needs the geometry �?" the exact check that withdrew
13618: `sgideoob` as a false OOB. Named, not judged.
13619: 
13620: **Single consumer: `machine_alpha.c:61`**, `device_add(machine, "lca")`. No alpha rig here �?"
13621: the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` escaped notice. No rig means no coverage, not no bug.
13622: 
13623: *Companion to `pcc2widthkills`, deliberately examined separately. Both surfaced from one question
13624: �?" is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? �?" but they are different classes: pcc2 is
13625: WIDTH kills, this is VALUE kills. Filing them as one row would merge two fix designs, and a
13626: census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*
13627: 
13628: ### `pcc2widthkills` (HIGH) -- twelve width kills, twice `#497`'s six, in a file nobody looked at
13629: 
13630: Every one is the same shape:
13631: 
13632: ```c
13633: case PCCTWO_GENCTL:
13634:         if (len != 1) {
13635:                 fatal("TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_GENCTL
13636: ");
13637:                 exit(1);
13638:         }
13639: ```
13640: 
13641: Twelve of them �?" lines 359, 371, 394, 407, 429, 467, 477, 492, 507, 528, 543, 563 �?" **all
13642: `len != 1`, all ending the host**, verified by walking each guard's block rather than grepping
13643: adjacency. Seventeen terminating calls in the file overall.
13644: 
13645: > *** CORRECTED 2026-09-03: TEN kills, not twelve.  Lines 543 and 563 are the IPL and MASK
13646: > arms, and both were ALREADY `#119` warn-once latches when this was written -- `if (!w) {
13647: > fatal(...); w = 1; } break;` -- not `exit(1)`.  "Verified by walking each guard's block"
13648: > was not true of those two.  The ten kill BLOCKS cover TWELVE register labels (TxCTL and
13649: > TxICR each carry two), which is probably where twelve came from.  The adjudication's
13650: > "5 of the 10" used the right number.  And the two `#119` latches are themselves the
13651: > `staticlatchscope` class and are folded into the fix. ***
13652: 
13653: **The same class `#337` fixed in `dev_wdc.c` and `#497` fixed in `dev_sgi_re.c`, at twice the
13654: site count.** The message says it plainly: *TODO: non-byte reads and writes*. The author knew
