16909:     }
16910: 
16911: So: **guest writes 1 to IOC_CONF (accepted), then performs any PCI configuration-space access, and
16912: the host process exits.**  Two guest accesses, both to registers the model implements, neither of
16913: them rejected.
16914: 
16915: *** WHY IT IS ITS OWN ROW AND NOT PART OF [[lcabadval]]. ***  Every kill `lcabadval` covers is a
16916: guard REFUSING a value it does not recognise.  **This one fires on a value the guard ACCEPTS**, in a
16917: different function, on a later access.  The acceptance and the kill are separated in both code and
16918: time, which is why a per-arm audit of `lca_ioc` walks straight past it -- as mine did, twice.
16919: 
16920: *** AND THE SEAT'S FRAMING NEEDED CORRECTING, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. ***  grok wrote
16921: that this exit becomes *"newly reachable from a CONF extra-bit write that also sets bit 0; today
16922: that write dies in the CONF guard first."*  That is true of an EXTRA-BIT write (e.g. `0x3`), and it
16923: made the kill sound like a CONSEQUENCE of adopting option A.  **It is not: `0x1` alone reaches it
16924: today, with no fix applied.**  A widens which CONF values arrive here; it does not open the door.
16925: The door is open now.  *Checked by reading both arms rather than by accepting the seat's sentence --
16926: which is this project's own rule, and the seat was right about the site while being wrong about the
16927: timing.*
16928: 
16929: **THE IRONY, and it is a design signal rather than a joke:** the `bus == 0` branch is the file's own
16930: graceful-degradation PRECEDENT -- a commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted --
16931: and [[lcabadval]] cites that very loop as a transferable in-tree warrant.  **The `else` five lines
16932: below it is a bare kill.**  Same function, opposite dispositions, no comment explaining why.
16933: 
16934: **REACHABILITY, stated honestly rather than assumed.**  Bit 0 is a bus SELECTOR, so a guest reaches
16935: this by selecting a bus the model does not implement and then doing config access -- plausible for
16936: firmware or an OS that probes beyond bus 0, and trivially reachable by a probe.  **Whether any
16937: shipped guest on `alphabook1` actually does it is NOT established here**, and the row does not claim
16938: it.  What is established is that no guard stands between an accepted write and the kill.
16939: 
16940: **WITNESS: a rung-3 probe is available and most of it already exists.**  `device_add(machine, "lca")`
16941: is `ST_ALPHABOOK1` ONLY (`machine_alpha.c`), and `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY drives a
16942: guest `stq`/`ldq` at `LCA_IOC_CONF` (`0xfffffc0180000020`, K0SEG | physical) on unmodified
16943: `alphabook1`.  This row needs one more session: `stq 0x1` to CONF, then a PCI config-space access.
16944: **Three traps that probe already paid for:** debugger `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger
16945: `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is NOT a guest instruction, so it does not witness; and
16946: HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`.
16947: 
16948: **DISPOSITION, not yet adjudicated:** it is the same de-fatalisation family, but the option set
16949: differs because the kill is not a value refusal -- the honest members here are DECLINE THE CONFIG
16950: ACCESS (return without dispatching, with a latched announce) or IMPLEMENT bus != 0.  **Note the
16951: architecturally correct error path is blocked for the same reason grok gave on the sibling row:
16952: `LCA_IOC_STAT0`/`STAT1` have no case at all and hit the `default:` arm's `exit(1)`, so LCA cannot
16953: report a device error through the register the header designates for it.**
16954: 
16955: sev HIGH: a guest-drivable host kill, reached by two ordinary accesses with no rejected value
16956: anywhere on the path.
16957: 
16958: 
16959: ### `lcairqstubkill` (high) -- filed 2026-09-07: the LCA interrupt handlers are
16960: ### `fatal()+exit(1)` with no guard, and DEVINIT wires the ISA/IDE bus straight to them
16961: 
16962: FILED 2026-09-07.  **NOT a new discovery of the SITE** -- [[lcabadval]]'s eleven-kill census already
16963: enumerated `:73` and `:85` and called them *"plausibly the most severe of the eleven"*.  What was
16964: missing is a ROW: the census names them inside a row titled for VALUE GUARDS, which is the census's
16965: own closing lesson (*a row named after one class hides the members of the others*), and
16966: `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` mentioned them **zero** times.
16967: 
16968: **THE SITE, verbatim -- there is no predicate at all:**
16969: 
16970:     void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
16971:     {
16972:             fatal("lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n");
16973:             exit(1);
16974:     }
16975: 
16976:     void lca_interrupt_deassert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
16977:     {
16978:             fatal("lca_interrupt_deassert: TODO\n");
16979:             exit(1);
16980:     }
16981: 
16982: *** THE WIRING, DERIVED BY READING `DEVINIT(lca)` -- AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS SEVERE. ***
16983: 
16984:     memset(&interrupt_template, 0, sizeof(interrupt_template));
16985:     interrupt_template.line = 0;
16986:     interrupt_template.name = interrupt_path;                        /*  "<machine>.lca"  */
16987:     interrupt_template.extra = d;
16988:     interrupt_template.interrupt_assert   = lca_interrupt_assert;
16989:     interrupt_template.interrupt_deassert = lca_interrupt_deassert;
16990:     interrupt_handler_register(&interrupt_template);
16991: 
16992:     bus_isa_init(devinit->machine, interrupt_path,
16993:         BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1, LCA_ISA_BASE, LCA_ISA_MEMBASE);
16994: 
16995: **The stubs are registered as the handlers for `<machine>.lca`, and `bus_isa_init` is handed THAT
16996: SAME PATH together with `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`.**  So the ISA bus -- both IDE controllers
16997: included -- routes its interrupts into a function whose entire body is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.  The
16998: file's own comment above the stub says *"Line 0 = ISA interrupt."*
16999: 
17000: **WHY IT IS PLAUSIBLY THE WORST KILL IN THE FILE.**  Every other terminating call in `dev_lca.c`
17001: needs a guest to write a value the model does not expect.  **This one needs only that an interrupt
17002: is asserted** -- i.e. ordinary disk activity on a machine whose DEVINIT attaches two IDE channels.
17003: No unusual value, no undocumented register, no firmware revision.
17004: 
17005: *** WHAT IS ESTABLISHED AND WHAT IS NOT -- read this before quoting the row. ***
17006: * **ESTABLISHED, by reading:** the stubs terminate unconditionally; they are registered as the
17007:   interrupt handlers; `bus_isa_init` receives the same interrupt path with both IDE channels.
17008: * **NOT ESTABLISHED:** that any guest actually reaches them.  **No measurement has been made.**  A
17009:   device could conceivably never assert on this path, and `interrupt_handler_register` semantics
17010:   have not been read here.  The row does NOT claim "alphabook1 cannot boot"; it claims the wiring
17011:   is present and the handler is a kill.
17012: 
17013: **NEXT STEP IS A RUNG-3 ATTEMPT, and the apparatus already exists.**
17014: `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) already boots
17015: unmodified `alphabook1` under the debugger with a disk image attached and drives guest `ldq`/`stq`
17016: through real decode.  The question is whether a guest access can make an IDE channel assert while
17017: single-stepping -- **and the carrier's rule cuts against it: a step-based probe sees a SYNCHRONOUS
17018: assert (a device handler reached inside the guest's own store) but NOT one that depends on a
17019: host-timer callback.**  So the honest first question is *what produces the assert*, and if the
17020: answer is `timer_add`, this needs a free-running probe rather than a stepped one.
17021: 
17022: **DISPOSITION: option-set MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT), and the set's other members do not apply.**  There
17023: is no value being refused and nothing to decline, accept, mask or fault -- it is an entire path
17024: stubbed out.  The carrier added member 7 on 2026-09-06 from this very file, using these very
17025: functions as its example.  A cheaper interim is member 3 (decline + LATCHED announce): return
17026: without asserting, announce once, and let the guest see a missing interrupt rather than a dead host
17027: -- **but that trades a host kill for a silently lost interrupt**, which is the readback-lie shape
17028: [[lcabadval]] is currently held on, so it is a design question and not an obvious win.
17029: 
17030: **TRANSFERABLE PRECEDENT, already closed in this tree:** [[lunaabsentcpu]] -- *"dev_luna88k.c
17031: reassert_interrupts() calls the NULL interrupt callback of an UNCONFIGURED cpu slot"* -- same family
17032: (a terminating/absent interrupt callback reached by ordinary operation), fixed, and closed with a
17033: rung-3 probe witness (`regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`).  Read that round before designing this one.
17034: 
17035: sev HIGH on the wiring; it would be CRITICAL if a witness lands, and the row says so rather than
17036: pre-judging it.
17037: 
17038: 
17039: ### `uninorthdevscan` (med, pending a reachability answer) -- filed 2026-09-07:
17040: ### a sign-extended mask can select the wrong PCI device on macppc
17041: 
17042: FILED 2026-09-07, split out of [[shiftubcensus]] item (A) because it is the only member of that
17043: census with a **GUEST-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE** rather than a language-level one, and it therefore owes a
17044: witness the others do not.
17045: 
17046: **THE SITE**, `dev_uninorth.c`, in the PCI address-register write path:
17047: 
17048:     :56    uint64_t idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len | MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN);
17049:     ...
17050:     :60    d->cur_addr = idata;
17051:     :70    for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)
17052:     :71        if (idata & (1 << dev))
17053:     :72            break;
17054: 
17055: **`idata` is `uint64_t` and `1 << dev` is an `int`.**  At `dev == 31` that is `0x80000000`, which as
17056: a signed `int` is NEGATIVE, so converting it for the `&` against a `uint64_t` **SIGN-EXTENDS it to
17057: `0xffffffff80000000`**.
17058: 
17059: > **CONSEQUENCE IF `idata` CARRIES ANY BIT IN 32..63: the loop matches at `dev == 31` when no
17060: > device bit is set there, and the scan selects the WRONG DEVICE.**  The repair (`1U << dev`) is

