FILE regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py
   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: #
   3: #  lcairqstubkill -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: ONE guest store to the 8042 command port ends the host.
   4: #
   5: #  THE DEFECT.  `dev_lca.c' registers the machine's interrupt handlers and they are bare kills:
   6: #
   7: #      void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
   8: #      {
   9: #              fatal("lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n");
  10: #              exit(1);
  11: #      }                                        /*  and _deassert, identically  */
  12: #
  13: #  `DEVINIT(lca)' registers them for "<machine>.lca" and hands that SAME path to `bus_isa_init',
  14: #  so every ISA interrupt is routed into a function whose whole body is fatal() + exit(1).
  15: #
  16: #  THE CHAIN, every link read in the source and then MEASURED by this probe:
  17: #
  18: #    1.  a guest store to the 8042 COMMAND port queues a reply; `dev_pckbc_access' ends by
  19: #        calling `pckbc_reassert_interrupts'                            -- SYNCHRONOUS, in the
  20: #                                                                          guest store itself
  21: #    2.  that asserts ISA line 1; `isa_interrupt_assert' sets the PIC IRR bit
  22: #    3.  `isa_interrupt_common': new = pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier.  `dev_8259.c' memsets the struct
  23: #        and `ier' is written ONLY by a guest store, so AT RESET NOTHING IS MASKED and the first
  24: #        IRR bit propagates upward on d->irq
  25: #    4.  d->irq came from INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path,...) = "<machine>.lca"
  26: #    5.  lca_interrupt_assert(): fatal(); exit(1)
  27: #
  28: #  **Four guest instructions total.  No disk image, no OS, no host timer, no driver setup, and no
  29: #  unusual value -- 0xAA is the standard 8042 self-test command.**
  30: #
  31: #  ROUTE CHOSEN FROM A codex ISA CENSUS (regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/codex_reach_20260907.txt,
  32: #  denominator: 12 instantiation call sites in bus_isa_init).  Two of its findings decided the
  33: #  shape, and both corrected a premise of mine:
  34: #    *  **the WDC controllers exist only if an IDE image is attached**, so the IDE route this row
  35: #       was originally written around is NOT available to a probe that attaches a memory file;
  36: #    *  **PIT and RTC asserts are HOST-TIMER driven** and therefore invisible to a stepped probe,
  37: #       while PCKBC and the UARTs are synchronous with the guest access.
  38: #
  39: #  THE MATCHED CONTROL IS THE LPT PORT, and the census is why it is the right one: `dev_lpt_tick'
  40: #  is EMPTY and `dev_lpt_access' never asserts an IRQ.  So a store of the same width, through the
  41: #  same K0SEG path, into the same ISA window, at a port that cannot assert, MUST SURVIVE.  That is
  42: #  what attributes the death to the DEVICE REACHED rather than to storing into ISA space at all.
  43: #
  44: #  ONE PROCESS, control first: only the PORT differs between the two stores.
  45: #
  46: #  *** CONTRACT -- WHAT THIS WITNESSES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. ***
  47: #  It establishes that on unmodified `alphabook1' a single ordinary guest store to a documented
  48: #  8042 register ends the host process, with the terminating stub's own diagnostic as evidence.
  49: #  It does NOT establish that any shipped guest issues that command, and it is NOT a detector of a
  50: #  repair: "host survives" would green for a silent return, for a latched announce, and for an
  51: #  actual interrupt implementation alike.  A fix round owes a row that discriminates those.
  52: #
  53: #  Usage:  lca_irqstub_probe.py <gxemul-binary> [label]
  54: import os, sys, pty, select, time, re
  55: 
  56: BIN   = sys.argv[1]
  57: LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "?"
  58: MACH  = "alphabook1"          #  device_add(machine, "lca") is ST_ALPHABOOK1 ONLY
FILE regress/lca_confbus_probe.py
   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: #
   3: #  lcaconfbus1kill -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: an ACCEPTED write to LCA_IOC_CONF arms a host kill in a
   4: #  DIFFERENT handler, reached by an ordinary guest load with no rejected value anywhere on the path.
   5: #
   6: #  THE DEFECT, two handlers in `dev_lca.c':
   7: #
   8: #      DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc), case LCA_IOC_CONF:
   9: #              d->ioc_conf = idata;
  10: #              /*  Only bit 0 is implemented so far, the PCI bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit.  */
  11: #              if (idata & ~1) { fatal(...); exit(1); }     <-- 0x1 PASSES.  Accepted and stored.
  12: #
  13: #      DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf):
  14: #              bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;
  15: #              if (bus == 0) { ...dev scan, already de-fatalised... }
  16: #              else { fatal("TODO. Non-zero bus.\n"); exit(1); }
  17: #
  18: #  So: guest stores 0x1 to IOC_CONF (accepted), then ANY access in the PCI config window ends the
  19: #  host.  Two ordinary accesses; nothing is refused.
  20: #
  21: #  *** WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT A SESSION IN lca_confwidth_probe.py. ***  That probe is a
  22: #  SHIPPED witness with its own contract (HEAD 4/6, fix 6/6) recorded in the wiring manifest.  Adding
  23: #  rows to a shipped detector perturbs its RESULT line and its recorded scores, and this defect is a
  24: #  separate queue row with a separate disposition.  The ENCODERS and the session driver are copied
  25: #  deliberately -- probes in this tree are standalone by design.
  26: #
  27: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  28: #  DESIGN REVIEWED BY THE grok SEAT, 2026-09-07, AFTER codex REFUSED THE BRIEF ON A CONTENT FILTER
  29: #  (regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/grok_witness_20260907.txt).  VERDICT: SOUND.  Four corrections
  30: #  from that review are built in here and are NOT optional:
  31: #
  32: #    1.  ONE PROCESS, NOT TWO SESSIONS.  The control read and the armed read happen in the SAME
  33: #        emulator run, so ONLY `ioc_conf' differs between them.  Two separate sessions would leave a
  34: #        residual that something else differed before the debugger prompt.
  35: #    2.  S2 IS A READ, NOT A WRITE.  Both reach the kill.  But an empty-slot WRITE makes
  36: #        `bus_pci_data_access' print "[ ... write to non-existant device ... ]", so the CONTROL
  37: #        would carry a second fatal() on stderr.  An empty-slot READ of reg 0 is the architectural
  38: #        ID probe and is silent.
  39: #    3.  LIVENESS IS NOT `ioc rt'.  That row (in the sibling probe) proves the WRITER round-trips;
  40: #        it says nothing about THIS window.  Liveness here is the control read SURVIVING and
  41: #        returning the empty-slot signature.
  42: #    4.  STAY ON `step'.  `continue' would make `lca_interrupt_assert'/`_deassert' -- bare
  43: #        fatal()+exit(1) stubs with no guard -- a confounder.
  44: #
  45: #  THREE TRAPS THE SIBLING PROBE ALREADY PAID FOR, and none recurs here:
  46: #    *  debugger `dump' skips DM_DEFAULT handlers and would return the #95 pre-zero, making both
  47: #       reads look unmapped.  Every access below is a guest instruction.
  48: #    *  a debugger `put d' of a killing value IS a host kill but is NOT a guest instruction, so it
  49: #       witnesses nothing.  The arming store is a guest `stq'.
  50: #    *  HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds -- but that is `LCA_IOC_BASE+0' in `lca_ioc'.
  51: #       `lca_pci_conf' has NO per-offset special case, so `LCA_PCI_CONF+0' is a clean control.
  52: #
  53: #  *** CONTRACT -- WHAT THIS WITNESSES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. ***
FILE regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py
   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: #
   3: #  lcaconfwidth -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: the LCA IOC_CONF kill diagnostic names a value the guard ACCEPTS.
   4: #
   5: #  THE DEFECT, dev_lca.c, `case LCA_IOC_CONF:'
   6: #
   7: #      d->ioc_conf = idata;                     /* the FULL 64-bit value is stored   */
   8: #      if (idata & ~1) {                        /* the guard sees all 64 bits        */
   9: #              fatal("TODO: Write to unimplemented bit of"
  10: #                  " IOC_CONF: 0x%x\n", (int)idata);   /* the message sees 32        */
  11: #              exit(1);
  12: #      }
  13: #
  14: #  A guest `stq' of 0x100000000 sets bit 32.  `idata & ~1' is true, so the process dies -- and
  15: #  `(int)idata' is 0, which is a value this guard ACCEPTS.  The kill message names an acceptable
  16: #  value while killing on an unacceptable one.  That is the whole defect, and it is observable.
  17: #
  18: #  *** WHAT THIS PROBE IS AND IS NOT.  It is a WITNESS (rung 3): it shows the symptom on the
  19: #  COMMITTED build, which is what licenses a fix.  It is not a detector and must not be gated --
  20: #  a witness stays red until the fix lands. ***
  21: #
  22: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  23: #  FOUR THINGS THE DESIGN REVIEW (grok, 2026-09-07) KILLED BEFORE THIS WAS WRITTEN.  Each would
  24: #  have produced a green or an unreadable run, and none is obvious from the source alone:
  25: #
  26: #  1. `load_const' CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants.
  27: #     The K0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  So both 64-bit words
  28: #     are planted in RAM with debugger `put d' and loaded with `ldq' while `ptbr' is still 0 --
  29: #     the same bootstrap alpha_harness_probe.py uses, and the only one that works.
  30: #
  31: #  2. DEBUGGER `dump' CANNOT BE THE READ-BACK.  `dump' calls memory_rw with NO_EXCEPTIONS, and
  32: #     LCA registers DM_DEFAULT rather than DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS, so the handler is
  33: #     SKIPPED and the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer.  That is the UNMAPPED SIGNATURE PRODUCED
  34: #     BY A HIT -- a false negative that looks exactly like a miss.  The read-back is a guest `ldq'.
  35: #
  36: #  3. `put d' OF THE KILLING VALUE IS NOT RUNG-3.  store_64bit_word goes through memory_rw with
  37: #     CACHE_DATA and no NO_EXCEPTIONS, so it DOES enter the handler and exit(1)s -- a real kill,
  38: #     caused by no guest instruction.  The killing store must be a guest `stq'.
  39: #
  40: #  4. A MISS IS NOT ONLY "UNMAPPED -> NOTHING".  Offset 0 of the window is LCA_IOC_BASE (HAE),
  41: #     which `break's with no kill.  A store to k0(0x180000000) is SILENT WHILE INSIDE the LCA
  42: #     window.  Unmapped and HAE both read back 0, so the control has to tell them apart -- which

