1875: > ### *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET -- adopted 2026-08-25, after a round chose between two
1876: > ### options while the third was already shipping ONE FILE UP ITS OWN CALL PATH. ***
1877: >
1878: > `#463` de-fatalised a device and framed the choice as *keep `fatal()` unlatched* versus
1879: > *delete it*. It deleted, and recorded honestly that this cost the unconditional diagnostic.
1880: > **But `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523` -- in the file that DISPATCHES to that very device --
1881: > already bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`**, reasoning that
1882: > *"probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign."* Same phenomenon, and
1883: > the fork's own answer was BOUND THE COUNT, KEEP THE MESSAGE.
1884: >
1885: > Worse: **this carrier already said so.** *"A guest-drivable `fatal()` needs a latch and not a
1886: > verbosity flag"* was standing rule before that round, and the two-membered framing never
1887: > consulted it.
1888: >
1889: > > *** THE SET HAS SIX MEMBERS, NOT THREE.  Grown twice, and BOTH TIMES BY A SEAT THAT WENT
1890: > > LOOKING IN THE TREE RATHER THAN REASONING -- which is the instruction below working. ***
1891: > >
1892: > > **1 DELETE  �  2 DECLINE (silent)  �  3 DECLINE + BOUNDED (LATCHED) ANNOUNCE  �
1893: > > 4 ACCEPT  �  5 FAULT  �  6 BACKED ACCEPT  �  7 IMPLEMENT**
1894: >
1895: > > ### *** GROWN A THIRD TIME, 2026-09-06, AND AGAIN BY A SEAT THAT WENT LOOKING IN THE TREE. ***
1896: > >
1897: > > * **7 IMPLEMENT** -- write the code.  **All six members above presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION**,
1898: > >   and that presumption fails for an UNIMPLEMENTED PATH.  `dev_lca.c`'s `lca_interrupt_assert` and
1899: > >   `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal("...TODO"); exit(1)` with **no guard at all**: there is no value
1900: > >   being refused and nothing to decline, accept or fault.  In the codex seat's words, *"declining an
1901: > >   interrupt leaves the machine incomplete."*  The tell that member 7 is the right one is a site with NO
1902: > >   PREDICATE -- a whole path stubbed out, rather than a guard rejecting a value.
1903: > >
1904: > > *** AND THE SAME PASS FOUND THAT **MEMBER 5 IS NOT UNIVERSALLY AVAILABLE**, which no earlier statement of
1905: > > this set said. ***  FAULT works because `memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access --
1906: > > **for MIPS and M88K.  There is no Alpha equivalent.**  So `dev_unreadable.c`, which `machine_sgi.c`
1907: > > installs ten times, does not transfer to an Alpha machine at all, and a round reaching for FAULT there owes
1908: > > Alpha exception delivery first.
1909: > >
1910: > > > **BEFORE CHOOSING A MEMBER, CHECK IT IS AVAILABLE ON THIS CPU.**  The set is a menu of dispositions, not
1911: > > > a menu of things every machine can do -- and the option-set rule's own advice (grep the fork for the
1912: > > > idiom) finds the idiom without telling you whether the machinery under it exists on your target.  That is
1913: > > > the warrant-transfer rule one level down: a precedent real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby
1914: > > > transferable.
1915: > >
1916: > > * **4 ACCEPT** -- the model ALREADY computes the right value and then discards it.  Delete
1917: > >   the kill, keep the value, NO latch: there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce
1918: > >   would ship a wrong record in product code (*"unimplemented; ignored"* about a read that
1919: > >   is implemented and is not ignored).  Found 2026-08-27 when the DC21285 datasheet showed
1920: > >   `+0x188` is IRQEnable, a documented read-only register the model answers correctly.
1921: > > * **5 FAULT** -- deliver the architectural bus error, by MODELLING THE SPACE AS UNMAPPED.
1922: > >   `src/devices/dev_unreadable.c` is a device whose entire access function is `return 0;`
1923: > >   ("returns an error for all reads/writes"), and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times so
1924: > >   absent hardware faults, because the PROM and NetBSD probe those regions and RELY on the
1925: > >   failure.  **This is why *`return 0` is not a decline* and *FAULT is a member* are both
1926: > >   true: it is not a refusal, it is a positive statement that nothing is there.**
1927: > > * **6 BACKED ACCEPT** -- invent storage and accept, the `mte_reg[]`/`de_reg[]` idiom.
1928: > >   Distinct from 4: nothing computes a value, so this FABRICATES one.  Governed by the
1929: > >   ADDITION LICENCE -- a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never "cannot hurt".
1930: > >
1931: > > *(A seventh, ALIAS/mirror -- partial address decode folding a high window onto a low one,
1932: > > which real hardware often does -- was named and dismissed for want of evidence.  An
1933: > > unmapped-space arm's option set generically contains it; name it before dismissing it.)*
1934: > >
1935: > > **Before choosing, GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM -- the tree's own precedents are
1936: > > part of the design space.**  Members 4, 5 and 6 were ALL found that way and none of them
1937: > > by argument.  **And grep for the SIBLING HANDLERS' disposition in the same file**: on
1938: > > `sgireunmapped` that showed the three siblings already declined without `exit(1)` and
1939: > > pristine upstream did too, making the arm under review the file's own outlier -- upstream
1940: > > residue rather than a policy.  *That is a far stronger warrant than "it is a host kill",
1941: > > and it is one `grep` away.*
1942: >
1943: > This is the premise-audit discipline aimed at **option-space completeness** instead of at
1944: > factual premises. A brief that lists two options gets a panel arguing about two options;
1945: > nobody proposes the member that was never named. *Five seats and an adjudicator debated the
1946: > two-membered set; the seat that found the third had gone looking in the tree.*
1947: > #### *** AND THE FOUNDING STORY WAS WRONG. THE THIRD MEMBER *WAS* NAMED -- ON PASS 1, IN A
FILE tools/CLAUDE.md
1980: >
1981: > The same answer also named `asicindexcase` on pass 1 -- *"post-switch `r`-use audit before
1982: > the edit lands; absence admits converting a host kill into a silent OOB write on declined
1983: > offsets"* -- which the round later filed as a discovery.
1984: >
1985: > *Two rounds of design argument, one filed row and one carrier rule, all of which existed in
1986: > a file on disk the whole time. This is the third recorded instance of the unread-review
1987: > class and by some distance the most expensive.*
1988: >
1989: > And it pays across a whole filed queue, not one device: every remaining `exit(` de-fatalisation
1990: > faces the same choice, and `floodclass` already records guest-drivable log floods as a CLASS.
1991: >
1992: > #### *** THE RULE'S ADVICE WAS RIGHT AND ITS EXEMPLAR WAS THE TREE'S ONE OUTLIER.
1993: > #### Corrected 2026-08-25 -- and it was corrected BY FOLLOWING THE RULE. ***
1994: >
1995: > The rule says *grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first
1996: > principles*. The first round to obey it did exactly that, on `wdcflood`, and the census
1997: > came back against the rule's own example.
1998: >
1999: > **`memory_rw.c:495-523` is a bounded diagnostic, but it is the ONLY counter-style bound in
2000: > the tree, and every property that makes it a bad model was verified by reading it:**
2001: >
2002: > * it is **not a `fatal()`** -- `debugmsg_cpu(..., VERBOSITY_DEBUG, ...)`;
2003: > * it is **DOUBLE-GATED**: `ENOUGH_VERBOSITY(SUBSYS_MEMORY, VERBOSITY_DEBUG) && n_trunc_msgs < 8`,
2004: >   and because `&&` short-circuits, **the counter never increments at default verbosity**. The
2005: >   bound almost never engages, because the message is already invisible;
2006: > * `static int n_trunc_msgs` is function-local -- **GLOBAL, shared by every device and CPU**,
2007: >   not per-instance;
2008: > * it prints **no give-up notice**.
2009: >
2010: > > **THE HOUSE IDIOM IS NOT "FIRST N THEN QUIET". IT IS `N = 1`, PER DEVICE INSTANCE, PLUS AN
2011: > > EXPLICIT SUPPRESSION MARKER IN THE MESSAGE TEXT** -- roughly 34 latch sites across 11 device
2012: > > files. So member 3 should be read as **DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE**, and the counter form is
2013: > > the exception that needs a warrant, not the template.
2014: > >
2015: > > *** THE MARKER'S SPELLING WAS NAMED WRONG HERE: this clause used to say the idiom is
2016: > > `(further occurrences suppressed)`, which is the MINORITY form. Censused 2026-08-26 across
2017: > > `src/devices/*.c`, by message text: ***
2018: > >
