2621: > well; an exemplar is a frozen claim about ONE INSTANCE, and instances move** -- code is
2622: > edited, rows are re-scoped, line numbers drift, and a row can be silently conflated with a
2623: > second defect. Nothing re-checks an example once it is written down, because it reads as
2624: > illustration rather than as an assertion.
2625: >
2626: > **So: an exemplar in this file is a DATED MEASUREMENT wearing the clothes of an
2627: > illustration.** When a round leans on one, re-derive it rather than cite it -- and if it has
2628: > decayed, correct it in place and keep the rule. All three today were caught that way, and in
2629: > all three the instruction survived contact while the example did not.
2630: 
2631: **Round scope (the reopening rule):** a witness makes a site ELIGIBLE, never mandatory. A round
2632: takes ONE site; a second joins only if it is witnessed, in the SAME device and SAME semantic class
2633: under the third doctrine arm (so one fix design covers both), and covered by the SAME detector
2634: with no new oracle. **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**
2635: 
2636: ### *** TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS LADDER, 2026-08-26, from `reprowitness`'s held stage. The
2637: ### ladder failed its OWN exemplar, and its own checker had already written down the second gap. ***
2638: 
2639: **(a) CLAUSE (ii) IS SCOPED TO ACCESS WITNESSES, RUNGS 3-4 - it was written as rung-2-4 and is
2640: UNSATISFIABLE AT RUNG 2.** Rung 2 admits machine construction for init-time defects and names the
2641: macppc heap OOB (#23). A construction-time OOB **performs no address decode and returns no device
2642: value**, so it cannot carry a control of the form "a known value returns through the same decode".
2643: The ladder's own exemplar could not satisfy the ladder's own clause, and had not been able to
2644: since the day both were written.
